The stack stores a full search state, including the current position, path text, and visited cells. This avoids recursive calls while still allowing the search to go back and try another direction.

Each state owns its visited array, so changing one branch cannot affect another branch. Results are sorted at the end to keep the required order.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    private static class State {
        int row;
        int col;
        String path;
        boolean[][] used;

        State(int row, int col, String path, boolean[][] used) {
            this.row = row;
            this.col = col;
            this.path = path;
            this.used = used;
        }
    }

    public List<String> findPath(int[][] grid) {
        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
        int n = grid.length;
        if (n == 0 || grid[0].length != n || grid[0][0] == 0 || grid[n - 1][n - 1] == 0) {
            return result;
        }

        int[] rowChange = {1, 0, 0, -1};
        int[] colChange = {0, -1, 1, 0};
        char[] letters = {'D', 'L', 'R', 'U'};
        Deque<State> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();

        boolean[][] startUsed = new boolean[n][n];
        startUsed[0][0] = true;
        stack.push(new State(0, 0, "", startUsed));

        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            State current = stack.pop();
            if (current.row == n - 1 && current.col == n - 1) {
                result.add(current.path);
                continue;
            }

            for (int k = 3; k >= 0; --k) {
                int nextRow = current.row + rowChange[k];
                int nextCol = current.col + colChange[k];

                if (nextRow < 0 || nextRow >= n || nextCol < 0 || nextCol >= n ||
                    grid[nextRow][nextCol] == 0 || current.used[nextRow][nextCol]) {
                    continue;
                }

                boolean[][] nextUsed = new boolean[n][n];
                for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
                    nextUsed[i] = current.used[i].clone();
                }
                nextUsed[nextRow][nextCol] = true;
                stack.push(new State(nextRow, nextCol,
                                     current.path + letters[k], nextUsed));
            }
        }

        Collections.sort(result);
        return result;
    }
}
```

For example, from `(0,0)`, a state can try down, save that branch, and later return to try right. The copied visited matrix makes those branches independent.

Time depends on the number of simple paths. The state copies use `O(n^2)` space each, and the stack can hold many states, so this version uses more memory than normal recursive backtracking.

A small backtracking search is enough here. The current cell is marked before exploring, and it is unmarked when the call returns so that the same cell can be used by another path.

The move order is `D, L, R, U`, which is the usual order for this problem. The checks also stop the search when the start or destination is blocked.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<string> findPath(vector<vector<int> > &grid) {
        vector<string> paths;
        int n = grid.size();
        if (n == 0 || grid[0].size() != n || grid[0][0] == 0 || grid[n - 1][n - 1] == 0) {
            return paths;
        }

        vector<vector<int>> used(n, vector<int>(n, 0));
        string current;
        int dr[4] = {1, 0, 0, -1};
        int dc[4] = {0, -1, 1, 0};
        char move[4] = {'D', 'L', 'R', 'U'};

        function<void(int, int)> dfs = [&](int row, int col) {
            if (row == n - 1 && col == n - 1) {
                paths.push_back(current);
                return;
            }

            used[row][col] = 1;
            for (int k = 0; k < 4; ++k) {
                int nextRow = row + dr[k];
                int nextCol = col + dc[k];

                if (nextRow >= 0 && nextRow < n && nextCol >= 0 && nextCol < n &&
                    grid[nextRow][nextCol] == 1 && !used[nextRow][nextCol]) {
                    current.push_back(move[k]);
                    dfs(nextRow, nextCol);
                    current.pop_back();
                }
            }
            used[row][col] = 0;
        };

        dfs(0, 0);
        return paths;
    }
};
```

A one-cell open grid returns one empty path because the rat is already at the destination. A blocked start or end returns no paths.

Time is proportional to the number of explored simple paths, plus the length of the returned paths. Extra space is `O(n^2)` for the visited grid and recursion.

Before building paths, this version checks which cells can reach the bottom-right corner. That reverse search ignores the current route, but it safely tells us that a cell with no connection to the destination can never be part of an answer.

The final DFS still keeps its own visited grid, so it does not repeat cells. The first search is just a useful filter for blocked areas and dead ends.

```go
import "sort"

func findPath(grid [][]int) []string {
    n := len(grid)
    if n == 0 || len(grid[0]) != n || grid[0][0] == 0 || grid[n-1][n-1] == 0 {
        return []string{}
    }

    canReach := make([][]bool, n)
    for i := range canReach {
        canReach[i] = make([]bool, n)
    }

    queue := [][2]int{{n - 1, n - 1}}
    canReach[n-1][n-1] = true
    directions := [][2]int{{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}}

    for head := 0; head < len(queue); head++ {
        row, col := queue[head][0], queue[head][1]
        for _, direction := range directions {
            nextRow := row + direction[0]
            nextCol := col + direction[1]
            if nextRow >= 0 && nextRow < n && nextCol >= 0 && nextCol < n &&
                grid[nextRow][nextCol] == 1 && !canReach[nextRow][nextCol] {
                canReach[nextRow][nextCol] = true
                queue = append(queue, [2]int{nextRow, nextCol})
            }
        }
    }

    answer := []string{}
    used := make([][]bool, n)
    for i := range used {
        used[i] = make([]bool, n)
    }
    path := []byte{}
    moves := [][3]interface{}{
        {1, 0, byte('D')},
        {0, -1, byte('L')},
        {0, 1, byte('R')},
        {-1, 0, byte('U')},
    }

    var dfs func(int, int)
    dfs = func(row, col int) {
        if row == n-1 && col == n-1 {
            answer = append(answer, string(path))
            return
        }

        used[row][col] = true
        for _, move := range moves {
            nextRow := row + move[0].(int)
            nextCol := col + move[1].(int)
            letter := move[2].(byte)

            if nextRow >= 0 && nextRow < n && nextCol >= 0 && nextCol < n &&
                grid[nextRow][nextCol] == 1 && canReach[nextRow][nextCol] &&
                !used[nextRow][nextCol] {
                path = append(path, letter)
                dfs(nextRow, nextCol)
                path = path[:len(path)-1]
            }
        }
        used[row][col] = false
    }

    dfs(0, 0)
    sort.Strings(answer)
    return answer
}
```

The reverse search marks every open cell connected to the destination. In the path search, a move into an unmarked cell is skipped immediately.

Time is still output-sensitive because every valid route must be produced. The reachability grid, queue, and visited grid use `O(n^2)` space, not counting the returned strings.

A normal BFS can also enumerate the answers if each queue item stores its own path and its own visited cells. This is different from ordinary maze reachability because two states at the same cell may still be different when they arrived through different routes.

The code changes neither the input grid nor the cells inside it. It only creates separate state data for the search.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
    struct State {
        int row;
        int col;
        string path;
        vector<vector<bool>> used;
    };

public:
    vector<string> findPath(vector<vector<int> > &grid) {
        vector<string> answer;
        int n = grid.size();
        if (n == 0 || grid[0].size() != n || grid[0][0] == 0 || grid[n - 1][n - 1] == 0) {
            return answer;
        }

        int dr[4] = {1, 0, 0, -1};
        int dc[4] = {0, -1, 1, 0};
        char letter[4] = {'D', 'L', 'R', 'U'};
        queue<State> q;

        vector<vector<bool>> startUsed(n, vector<bool>(n, false));
        startUsed[0][0] = true;
        q.push({0, 0, "", startUsed});

        while (!q.empty()) {
            State current = q.front();
            q.pop();

            if (current.row == n - 1 && current.col == n - 1) {
                answer.push_back(current.path);
                continue;
            }

            for (int k = 0; k < 4; ++k) {
                int nextRow = current.row + dr[k];
                int nextCol = current.col + dc[k];

                if (nextRow < 0 || nextRow >= n || nextCol < 0 || nextCol >= n ||
                    grid[nextRow][nextCol] == 0 || current.used[nextRow][nextCol]) {
                    continue;
                }

                vector<vector<bool>> nextUsed = current.used;
                nextUsed[nextRow][nextCol] = true;
                q.push({nextRow, nextCol, current.path + letter[k], nextUsed});
            }
        }

        sort(answer.begin(), answer.end());
        return answer;
    }
};
```

For `[[1]]`, the queue starts at the destination and returns `{ "" }`. If either corner is `0`, it returns an empty vector. Those cases are checked before the queue is created.

Time grows with the number of route states and the cost of copying their visited grids. The search uses `O(n^2)` space per stored state, in addition to the queue and output.

